A||||4||Emory Snyder||1987 rear brakes||||8||
Z||000000||Emory Snyder||06-25-2000||08:00 PM||||Can someone please tell me what is wrong with my right rear brake?  The caliper is stuck and won't release until you open the bleeder valve.  This is the third time that I have replaced the caliper with a remanufactured one from Advanced Auto(in as many days). Could it be something other than the caliper? The hose looks fine and fluid flows through it ok.||||reg||8
Z||000001||88formula||06-25-2000||09:49 PM||||Assuming that the e-brake system is functioning properly adjusted right, and the spring on the back of the caliper is there than you may have a bad proportioning valve. The proportioning valve limits the brake pressure to the rear brakes so that they don't lock up in the rear. You could have a kinked line also. Switch the two proportioning valves around to see if the symptom follows the valve. <P>If it were the master cylinder then the left front caliper would exhibit the same symptom as the rear because the hydraulic system is diagonally split. This means that one rear and one front caliper on opposite sides of the car share one of the two pistons in the master cylinder.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-25-2000||10:31 PM||||I'm guessing something is screwed up with the parking brake cable/adjustment, or the e-brake caliper lever and/or spring isn't getting installed correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Emory Snyder||06-27-2000||06:19 PM||||Thanks for the info- talked to two GM mechanics (one a fiero owner) and they both said it can't be combination valve as both rear brakes would lock-up(one line comes off valve and goes to a "T").  Pontiac mech. said it probably is a collapsed hose; I'll try that next I guess(he told me if I open bleeder and brake releases before I told him what was happening ).  E-brake was not installed (just the return spring )during last test of brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||-=shame=-||06-28-2000||08:36 AM||||Definetly Hose, only prob is, the calpier may have been cooked. BBQ caliper anyone, MMMMMMMM. <BR>||||reg||1
